Run Subfinder (if you haven’t already):
      subfinder -d example.com -silent -o subs.txt
or with more sources
   subfinder -d example.com -all -silent -o subs.txt
   
Check for alive hosts with httpx:
      cat subs.txt | httpx -silent -o alive.txt
   
   Or in one line:
      subfinder -d example.com -silent | httpx -silent -o alive.txt

Recommended Useful Flags for httpx

cat subs.txt | httpx -silent -status-code -title -tech-detect -follow-redirects -o alive.txt

-status-code / -sc → shows HTTP status (200, 301, 403, etc.)
-title → page title
-tech-detect / -td → detects technologies
-follow-redirects → follows redirects
-threads 50 (or higher) → speed it up
-timeout 5 → adjust timeout if needed
-rl 10 → rate-limit (good for polite scanning)

Example richer output:
httpx -l subs.txt -silent -sc -title -td -o live_hosts.txt

Optional: First Resolve DNS (with dnsx)

If you only want hosts that resolve (before checking web response):

cat subs.txt | dnsx -silent -a -resp -o resolved.txt
cat resolved.txt | httpx -silent -o alive.txt

Or combined:
subfinder -d example.com -silent | dnsx -silent | httpx -silent -sc -title -o alive.txt

Installation (if needed)

Both tools are from ProjectDiscovery:

go install -v github.com/projectdiscovery/subfinder/v2/cmd/subfinder@latest
go install -v github.com/projectdiscovery/httpx/cmd/httpx@latest
go install -v github.com/projectdiscovery/dnsx/cmd/dnsx@latest

Make sure ~/go/bin is in your $PATH.

Quick One-Liner Script Example

#!/bin/bash
DOMAIN=$1
subfinder -d $DOMAIN -silent -o subs_$DOMAIN.txt
cat subs_$DOMAIN.txt | httpx -silent -status-code -title -o live_$DOMAIN.txt
echo "Live hosts saved to live_$DOMAIN.txt"


